Correspondence.ResponseSummary:
-From Garret Dalton, Director of Operations: Island Express Helicopters Inc. (“Island Express”) is in receipt of your letter dated February 25, 2021, which references two safety recommendations stemming from the NTSB report entitled Rapid Descent Into Terrain, Island Express Helicopters Inc., Sikorsky S-76B, N72EX, Calabasa, California, January 26, 2020 (“NTSB Report”). While we dispute a number of the findings and conclusions in the NTSB Report, safety is our core value at Island Express, and we have carefully considered the recommendations as a part of our constant commitment to safety.

Pursuant to your request, we are writing this letter to detail the steps that Island Express has taken and intends to take in order to address the NTSB’s recommendations.

In Safety Recommendation A-21-8 the NTSB recommends that Island Express “[i]nstall flight data recording devices capable of supporting a flight data monitoring (FDM) program on each helicopter in [its] fleet and establish an FDM program that reviews all available data sources to identify deviations from established norms and procedures as well as other potential safety issues.” Consistent with this recommendation, Island Express is currently working to implement an FDM program and to integrate that program into its SMS. Island Express is evaluating two vendors for its FDM program: 1) the “Spidertracks” flight data monitoring & aircraft tracking system (www.spidertracks.com); and 2) the “Appareo” flight data monitoring system (www.appareo.com). We anticipate that this selection and implementation process will be completed soon.

In addition to the efforts made to address the specific Safety Recommendations referenced in your letter, we want to further inform you of our efforts to address other issues identified within the NTSB Report. As part of its analysis, the NTSB Report referenced the following “safety issues:”

• The pilot’s preflight weather and flight risk planning (section 2.2.1);

• The flight’s entry into IMC (section 2.2.2), and the pilot’s inadequate adverse weather avoidance (section 2.2.2.2);

• The pilot’s spatial disorientation (section 2.2.3);

• Factors influencing the pilot’s decision to continue the flight into adverse weather, including his self-induced pressure, lack of an alternate plan, and plan continuation bias (section 2.3); and

• The benefits of flight simulation devices for pilot training in adverse weather avoidance (section 2.5).

Although these items were not incorporated into the specific Safety Recommendations, and although we dispute a number of the items identified in the NTSB Report, Island Express has taken steps to address these items, including:

• We have updated our General Operating Manual (“GOM”) (effective 03-06-20) - Operational Control - changed Flight Safety Officer to Safety Officer; Section R Loss of Control (“LOC”) IIMC avoidance policy;

• We have updated our Pilot Training Manual (effective 03-06-20) - Chapter B – added Unusual Attitude Avoidance and Recovery, Inadvertent Entry to IMC; Chapter F – added Brownout, Whitelight and Flatlight, Unusual Attitude and Inadvertent Entry to IMC;

• All pilots have received training on our LOC & IIMC avoidance policy, which includes checking weather, personal weather minimums, alternates & exercising alternates or diverting, precautionary landings, and, lastly, if entering IIMC in emergency how to transition to IFR as per our Pilot Training Manual;

• We have implemented an annual simulator training program for all S-76 pilots, which is scheduled to begin in October 2021 (the first-available class) at CAE, Morristown NJ; and

• The pilot Flight Risk Assessment Tool (“FRAT”) has been updated, and all pilots have received documented training on the updated FRAT.

Additionally, we were disappointed that the NTSB did not take the opportunity to make specific Safety Recommendations to the FAA to address safety issues regarding the performance of air traffic control (“ATC”) and to recommend policy and training changes necessary to ensure proper termination of radar services, communications with pilots regarding the termination of radar services, proper pass down relief briefings, and the utilization of checklists in pass down relief briefings, among other things. The performance of ATC personnel was clearly not consistent with the requirements of Order JO 7110.65Y, which was among the references listed in the NTSB Report. ATC training and performance certainly falls within the scope of issues the NTSB has traditionally sought to address through the Safety Recommendation process (i.e. Safety Recommendations A-16-018 and A-16-019). Although the FAA claims to have provided updated training in April 2019 to address issues such as inadvertent flight into instrument meteorological conditions; control difficulties; recognizing emergencies; determining what help is needed; and taking actions that help pilots safely resolve the situation, it appears that the FAA’s required training does not reflect the best practices (or even the basic practices) for properly managing the National Airspace System or for providing ATC services to pilots. The FAA is in the best position to address such issues, and the NTSB should not miss the opportunity to exercise its Congressional charge and make the necessary recommendations to the FAA in order to prevent future accidents and save lives.

We trust that the efforts referenced above and our plans for the future demonstrate that we share the NTSB’s interest in eliminating the risk of aviation accidents and maximizing safety. If you have any questions about the information presented in this letter, please do not hesitate to contact our office.

-From Garret Dalton, Director of Operations: Island Express Helicopters Inc. (“Island Express”) is in receipt of your letter dated February 25, 2021, which references two safety recommendations stemming from the NTSB report entitled Rapid Descent Into Terrain, Island Express Helicopters Inc., Sikorsky S-76B, N72EX, Calabasa, California, January 26, 2020 (“NTSB Report”). While we dispute a number of the findings and conclusions in the NTSB Report, safety is our core value at Island Express, and we have carefully considered the recommendations as a part of our constant commitment to safety.

Pursuant to your request, we are writing this letter to detail the steps that Island Express has taken and intends to take in order to address the NTSB’s recommendations.

The NTSB report contains an extensive recitation of its position on the usefulness of Safety Management Systems (“SMS”), and its recommendation that the Federal Aviation Administration (“FAA”) make SMS mandatory for Part 135 operators. As discussed within the Report, the FAA has consistently refused to make SMS mandatory by regulation. The NTSB Report states that the “NTSB believes that Island Express should not wait for the FAA to take action on requiring SMS for Part 135 operators before fully implementing its SMS,” and NTSB Safety Recommendation A-21-7 recommends that Island Express “[p]articipate in the Federal Aviation Administration’s Safety Management System Voluntary Program.”

Contrary to the implication of recommendation A-21-7, Island Express has not waited for the FAA to mandate the use of SMS by Part 135 operators. Island Express has utilized an industry approved, third-party SMS since 2013, and has constantly worked with PRISM, an industry leading SMS vendor that uses FAA and international standards and guidance, to develop, implement and refine its SMS. Island Express’ SMS is outlined within its “Safety Management System Manual.” The structure of the SMS is consistent with FAA standards, including safety policy, safety risk management, safety assurance, and safety promotion. As part of its ongoing commitment to SMS refinement and improvement, Island Express has taken the following additional steps to further enhance its existing SMS:

• The company Safety Manager attended the PRISM SMS Safety Manager Training in September 2020;

• The company Safety Manager received PRISM Safety Assurance Program training;

• The company Safety Manager has started a new Internal Evaluation Program, which will include flight operations, dispatch, maintenance programs, and management oversight;

• The company Safety Manager has enacted a new company Safety Assurance Program;

• All company Hazard Reports are given a 5-Step Risk Management Process, they are given a Risk Matrix Score, corrective actions are administered, and the Hazard Reports are published and made available to all company personnel;

• All company Safety Meetings are now held on a monthly basis, which includes company ground crew, pilots, and mechanics;

• The company has updated all Risk Assessments for ground crews, pilots, and mechanics; and

• The company has updated SMS training for all employees.

Island Express will continue to work with PRISM on a routine basis to further refine its SMS program in accordance with industry and governmental standards and best practices.

-From Steve Dickson, Administrator: The FAA is currently preparing a letter to send directly to the AWOS vendors and manufacturers notifying them of the results of NTSB Report ASR-21-01, Revise Processes, Procedures, and Reporting Capabilities for Automated Weather Systems. In the letter, the FAA will highlight the following:
• The problem identified within the report;
• Applicable verbiage from Safety Recommendation A-21-4;
• A reference to FAA Order 7930.2S and the policy with respect to AWOS time inaccuracy;
• A request to submit a plan within 90 days to mitigate the issue; and
• A notice that the FAA plans to revise Advisory Circular (AC) 150/5220-16E, AWOS for Non-Federal Applications, to increase the frequency for validating and updating the system’s clock.

The AC revision and review cycle typically requires approximately 18 months to complete. Upon publication of the AC revision, the updated clock parameter will be added to the Non-Federal AWOS inspection checklist.

I will keep the Board informed of the FAA’s progress on these recommendations and anticipate providing an update by May 31, 2022.

-From Steve Dickson, Administrator: The Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) initiated a human factors-based research and development project investigating the effectiveness of novel lighting systems making closed parallel runways more conspicuous to pilots. The project will examine the effectiveness of several variations to the standard lighted X method described in Advisory Circular (AC) 150/5345-55A, Specification for L-893, Lighted Visual Aid to Indicate Temporary Runway Closure.

These lighting variations include:
• An additional lighted X fixture set 1,000 feet down the runway from the approach threshold;
• Two lighted Xs at the same location as recommended in the AC;
• Two or more lighted Xs side by side at the threshold or along the runway centerline;
• Varying the flash rates from the standard 2.5 seconds;
• Sequentially progressive flashing of multiple lighted Xs;
• Different size or colors for the lighted Xs; and
• Use of light emitting diode or halogen bulbs.

This project started on September 9, 2019, and the schedule includes time for selecting research methods, subject matter experts, developing test plans, executing test plans, data collection, and preparing the final report. The anticipated completion date is February 25, 2022. Additionally, the FAA’s Surface Safety Group continues to address wrong surface events of all kinds.

I will keep the Board informed of the FAA’s progress on this safety recommendation and anticipate providing an update by March 31, 2022.

-From Billy Nolen, Acting Administrator: The Federal Aviation Administration’s (FAA) Alaska communications requirements are ranked nationally among other National Airspace System (NAS) requirements competing for Communication Facilities Sustainment funding mentioned in our previous letter. We considered the following in determining the prioritization of the communications requirement need: 

1. Congressionally mandated or Office of the Secretary of Transportation directed. 

2. Urgent or immediate operational impact (i.e., requirements critical to NAS operations, prevents the accomplishment of an operational requirement that degrades safety).

Requirement Criteria: 
i.	Regional ranking; and 
ii.	Justification is based upon the following NAS operational factors: 
a. Aircraft near-miss event (safety); 
b. Delays; 
c. Facility relocation (lease terminated or terminating during the current fiscal year); 
d. Business case; and e. Approved unsatisfactory condition report. 

3. Significant and non-operational sensitive (i.e., requirements that affect the efficiency and capacity of the NAS). 
Requirement Criteria: 
i.	Regional ranking; and 
ii.	Sufficient justification based upon NAS efficiency and capacity factors: 
a. Coverage [en route/terminal/Automated Flight Service Station (AFSS)]; 
b. Congestion (en route/terminal/AFSS); 
c. Facility relocation (lease expiration within three years or due to airport expansion); 
d. Frequency interference; 
e. Diversity; 
f. Operations cost reduction/cost benefit; and 
g. Equipment enhancement.

Subject:
TO THE FEDERAL AVIATION ADMINISTRATION:  In collaboration with your internal offices that investigate aviation accidents and incidents and also organizations familiar with the challenges facing pilots confronting an emergency situation, annually revise the required training described in Safety Recommendation A-16-18 at the national level to ensure that the training is current and relevant and includes lessons learned from recent events throughout the National Airspace System that address best practices for helping pilots who are experiencing problems such as (but not limited to) the following:
(1) partial or total power loss and fuel emergencies;
(2) control difficulties;
(3) impaired navigational capability;
(4) loss of flight instruments or other critical aircraft systems (including attitude information);
(5) encounters with hazardous meteorological conditions;
(6) inadvertent flight into instrument meteorological conditions by visual flight rules pilots; and
(7) pilot medical issues, such as hypoxia.

Correspondence.ResponseSummary:
-From Steve Dickson, Administrator: Representatives from A TO will continue to work with government and industry stakeholders in aviation safety to identify best practices for assisting pilots experiencing problems. Where necessary, we will develop best practices checklists and make them accessible at air traffic facilities. Although not exhaustive, as there may be unlisted scenarios that can
occur, these checklists include the following:
• Engine Failure Best Practices;
• Hydraulic Failure;
• Icing Emergency Best Practices;
• Medical Emergency Procedures;
• No Radio/NORDO Procedures;
• Pressurization Best Practices;
• Identify Controller Responsibilities for Assisting Pilots [Instrument Flight Rules
(IFR)Nisual Flight Rules (VFR)]; and
• Identify Duty Priorities Regarding IFRNFR Operations.

I believe the FAA has effectively addressed these safety recommendations and consider our actions complete.

-From Steve Dickson, Administrator: Subject matter experts from the Federal Aviation Administration's (FAA) Air Traffic Organization (A TO) and Flight Standards Service met with representatives from the National Air Traffic Controllers Association and General Aviation Joint Steering Committee to review a training project addressed in this recommendation. The group recommended national recurrent training, delivered in a combined web-based and instructor-led discussion format, as the most effective way to train air traffic controllers. The FAA developed and delivered National Recurrent Training on Emergencies in April 2019. This training focused on the following topics:

• Identifying emergency procedures;
• Recognizing emergency situations;
• Recognizing the procedures for providing assistance to pilots in distress;
• Identifying the procedures for controlling aircraft in emergencies; and
• Identifying Team Resource Management duties related to emergency situations.

-From Kensuke Takeuchi, General Manager, Mitsubishi Heavy Industries America, Inc., Aircraft Product Support Division: Further to my letter of November 6, 2014, I am writing to report the progress Mitsubishi Heavy Industries America, Inc. (MHIA) ha~ made in responding to Safety Recommendation
A-14-99. As we previously mentioned, MHIA has already met with the FAA Fort Worth Aircraft Certification Office and outlined a program to implement the Safety Recommendation. Although to date, MHIA has not received formal acknowledgment and concurrence from the FAA, we have pressed on and have completed much of our proposed program. Currently the following has been accomplished:
1. MHIA has written and published a Service News (enclosed) witl1 distribution to every owner, operator, and pilot ofMU-2 series airplanes who registered with the FAA or who we are aware of whether or not they are on the FAA registry and to provided them with copies of Safety Recommendation A -14-99 as well as our strong recommendation that they fully comply with this Safety Recommendation;
2. MHIA is reviewing the responses and, if a response to our Service News pursuant to 1 above has not been received, we will follow-up with another letter to be sent through U.S. mails "Reply Receipt Requested";
3. MHIA has also written a separate letter to every known MU-2B i:nstmctor (enclosed) "Reply Receipt Requested" which provides such instructor with a copy of this Safety Recommendation and our strong recommendation that such instructor fully comply with the requirements of this Safety Recommendation. A compliance reply card was also enclosed;
4. Depending on the response to 3 above, we will follow-up with telephone calls and, if feasible, personal visits to ensure that all instructors are fully aware of the importance of this Safety Recommendation;
5. As you may know, MHI in conjunction with Turbine Aircraft Services ("TAS") conduct a biennial Pilot Review of Proficiency ("PROP") seminar in several locations across the country in which we present safety tips to all attendees along with various talks and discussions to encourage the safe operation of all MU-2 airplanes. As part of our PROP 2016 literature and presentations, we will emphasize the importance of compliance with the Safety Recommendation;
6. Our PROP Committee has agreed to invite members of the FAA and NTSB to attend PROP and we will provide the time necessary for a FAA presentation on the importance of compliance with this Safety Recommendation;
7. During the Operator Conferences slated for October 9thand 10th of 20l5, MHIA and
TAS are prepared to make similar presentations and afford the F AA/NTSB the opportunity to emphasize the importance of compliance with this Safety Recommendation.
8. On January 14, 2015, a conference call was made between MHIA, TAS and all MU-2 Authorized Service Centers. In addition to regular business, the NTSB Recommendation A-14-99 was discussed. Agreement was reached that, in the event an unapproved checklist was found in an MU-2 that was in for maintenance, the MU-2 Service Centers would assist in advising MU-2 Owners and Operators of the SF AR 108 checklist requirements.

We look forward to continue working with the FAA in this regard and to receiving the FAA's suggestions/concurrence as to how to best implement this Safety Recommendation. As our work with the FAA progresses, we will provide you with more details of the actions we have taken or intend to take to implement this important Safety Recommendation.

-From Kensuke Takeuchi, General Manager: I acknowledge receipt of your Safety Recommendation dated October 23, 2014 recommending that Mitsubishi Heavy Industries America, Inc. ("MHIA") "work with the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) to develop and distribute written guidance to flight instructors, owners, operators, and pilots of Mitsubishi MU-2B series airplanes about the requirement to use FAA-accepted checklists as specified in Special Federal Aviation Regulation No. 108. (A-14-99)."

MHIA is in full agreement with this Safety Recommendation and has already contacted and met with the FAA Aircraft Certification Office (ACO) in Fort Worth, Texas to discuss possible action items. It is our understanding, after this initial meeting with the FTW ACO, that the proposed action items that were forthcoming from our meeting, will be forwarded to Flight Standards (AFS 800) in Washington D.C. for consideration and concurrence to develop a program to implement the Safety Recommendation.

As you know, MHIA, as a manufacturer, does not have the authority to compel training or to enforce Federal Aviation Regulations or Safety Recommendations. However, over the past several years, we have taken many steps to encourage instructors, owners, operators, and pilots of MU-2B series airplanes to utilize and to comply with the FAA-accepted checklists as specified in Special Federal Aviation Regulation No. 108 (SPAR 108). We welcome this opportunity to work with the FAA to further encourage the use of only FAA-accepted checklists as set forth in SF AR 108. To accomplish this, we are proposing and have proposed to the FAA the following:
1. MHIA plans to issue a Service News that will be sent to every owner, operator, and pilot of MU-2 series airplanes who are registered with the FAA or who we are aware of whether or not they are on the FAA Aircraft Registry and to provide them with copies of Safety Recommendation A-14-99 as well as our strong recommendation that they fully comply with this Safety Recommendation;
2. MHIA has a MU-2 webpage on which we have already posted Safety Recommendation A-14-99 as well as our strong recommendation that the MU-2 owners, operators and pilots comply with this Safety Recommendation;
3. MHIA will write a letter to every known MU-2B instructor "Return Receipt Requested" and provide such instructor with a copy of this Safety Recommendation and our strong recommendation that such instructor fully comply with the requirements of this Safety Recommendation. A compliance reply card will also be enclosed;

If we do not receive a response to 3 above, we will follow-up with telephone calls and, if feasible, personal visits to ensure that all instructors are fully aware of the importance of this Safety Recommendation; As you may know, MHIA in conjunction with Turbine Aircraft Services ("T AS") conduct a biennial Pilot Review of Proficiency ("PROP") seminar in several locations across the country in which we present safety tips to all attendees along with various talks and discussions to encourage the safe operation of all MU-2 airplanes. Since 2008, we have specifically informed, recommended and encouraged the use of only official FAA-accepted checklists to the attendees at PROP. While we invite the entire MU-2 community to attend PROP, only about 50 percent actually attend. At future PROP events we intend to provide all attendees with this Safety Recommendation as part of our PROP literature and we will emphasize the importance of compliance during our presentations; Additionally, we traditionally invite members of the FAA and NTSB to attend PROP and we would be happy to afford appropriate employees of the F AA/NTSB the time necessary for presentations on the importance of compliance with this Safety Recommendation; During alternate years to PROP, MU-2 owners conduct Operator Conferences during which MHIA and TAS are prepared to make similar presentations and afford the FAA/NTSB the opportunity to emphasize the importance of compliance with this Safety Recommendation; During MU-2 Authorized Service Center meetings, MHIA plans to request that the service centers assist in informing the MU-2 Owners, Operators and Pilots that only the SPAR 108 accepted checklist should be used; and, We will recommend to the FAA, that more frequent ramp checks by FAA Flight Standards District Office personnel be conducted on MU-2s that are in their district to ensure compliance with SPAR 108, and more particularly, the checklist provisions of that rule.

We look forward to working with the FAA in this regard and to receiving the FAA's suggestions as to how to best implement this Safety Recommendation. As our work with the FAA progresses, we will provide you with more details of the actions we have taken or intend to take to implement this important Safety Recommendation.

-From Michael P. Huerta, Administrator: The Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) agrees with the Board's assessment that guidance should be developed to mitigate the unique hazards inherent in agricultural aircraft operations. We will work closely with the National Agricultural Aviation Research & Education Foundation to develop and distribute agricultural aircraft operations-specific guidance on:
• Agricultural aircraft inspection;
• Part 137 knowledge test subject areas and skills test items, with special emphasis on aerodynamic stall onset awareness and prevention;
• Fatigue, fatigue management strategies, and scheduling practices:
• Risk assessment and mitigation strategies;
• Best practices for performing, recording, and tracking mandatory and recommended maintenance items; and
• Quality assurance programs.

I will keep the Board informed of the FAA's progress on these recommendations and provide an updated response by July 31, 2015.

