-From Steve Dickson, Administrator: As previously stated, the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) has actively participated with the National Agricultural Aviation Research & Education Foundation Program Development Committee on the planning of Professional Aerial Applicators Support System programs that contain risk assessments in the human factors modules, such as fatigue and stress.

We also created brochures and safety bulletins on risk assessment and risk mitigation that were distributed in the 2016 and 2017 educational years. This included material on flying distractions such as: cell phones, watching videos, and other cockpit related distractions. This outreach will continue into 2020.

In our previous response on December 21, 2017, the FAA scheduled publication of a Safety Alert for Operators (SAFO) Best Practices for Title 14 of the Code of Federal Regulations (14 CFR) Part 137 Agricultural Aircraft Operations in Spring of 2018. The publication of the SAFO was delayed due to unforeseen additional complexities with incorporating all six topics.

The FAA published SAFO 20004, Best Practices for Title 14 of the Code of Federal Regulations (14 CFR) Part 137 Agricultural Aircraft Operations on March 20, 2020. SAFO 20004 includes best practices for part 137, which addresses six topics:
1. Information and checklist for performing preflight and in-flight site surveys, with special emphasis on attention management and obstacle collision avoidance strategies;
2. Information on the effects of density altitude;
3. Crosswind and awareness;
4. Aircraft weight and balance on aircraft performance during takeoff, landing, and while maneuvering;
5. Fuel management; and
6. Aerodynamic stall awareness and avoidance.

I believe the FAA has effectively addressed Safety Recommendation A-14-25 and consider our actions complete.

