-From Michael P. Huerta, Administrator: The Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) has met with the National Agricultural Aviation Association, the National Agricultural Aviation Research & Education Foundation (NAAREF), and the Professional Aerial Applicators Support System (P AASS) in April2015, December 2015, and February 2016 to discuss information and guidance that mitigate the unique hazards inherent in agricultural aircraft operations, such as:
• Agricultural aircraft inspection;
• Fatigue, fatigue management strategies, and scheduling practices;
• Risk assessment and mitigation strategies;
• Best practices for performing, recording, and tracking mandatory and recommended maintenance items; and
• Quality assurance programs.

The consensus of these meetings was that many agricultural aircraft operators are unaware of the availability and location of information to maintain their aircraft in an airworthy condition. As a result of the most recent meetings, the FAA is working closely with NAAREF and PAASS to identify the areas of concern and "ways forward," while P AASS agreed to issue videos, newsletters, and other pieces of information to address these areas of concern.

As noted in our previous letter dated August 10,2015, the FAA agreed to publish an Information for Operators (InFO) documenting the location of information pertinent to the inspection, alteration, and maintenance of agricultural aircraft to include regulatory references. The FAA is in the process of developing this InFO to enhance awareness and training for maintenance quality assurance and inspection programs for agricultural operations. This document will contain numerous suggestions on areas to ascertain information aimed at reducing the risk of accidents caused by improper maintenance practices, and we anticipate publication no later than December 31, 2016.

I will keep the Board informed of the FAA's progress on this recommendation and provide an update by June 30, 2017.

-From Michael P. Huerta, Administrator: On April 15, 2015, the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) met with the National Agricultural Aviation Association (NAAA), the National Agricultural Aviation Research & Education Foundation (NAAREF), and the Professional Aerial Applicators Support System (PAASS) to discuss information and guidance that mitigate the unique hazards inherent in agricultural aircraft operations, such as:
• Agricultural aircraft inspection;
• Fatigue, fatigue management strategies, and scheduling practices;
• Risk assessment and mitigation strategies;
• Best practices for performing, recording, and Tacking mandatory and recommended maintenance items; and
• Quality assurance programs.
The consensus of the meeting was that many agricultural aircraft operators are unaware of the availability and location of information to maintain their aircraft in an airworthy condition. To facilitate, the FAA agreed to publish an Information for Operators (InFO) documenting the location of information pertinent to the inspection, alteration, and maintenance of agricultural aircraft to include regulatory references. NAAA, NAAREF, and PAASS agreed to disseminate the InFO to their members using various methods available through their organizations.

We anticipate publication of an InFO by December 31, 2015. I will keep the Board informed of the FAA's progress on this safety recommendation and provide an update by February 2016.

-From Steve Dickson, Administrator: In response to this recommendation, the FAA published a revision to the Flight Standardization Board (FSB) report for the Embraer EMB-135, EMB-140, and EMB-145 airplanes on September 4, 2019 (attached). The report includes ground and flight training information with a special emphasis on “steering system inoperative and uncommanded swerving during critical phases of landing and takeoff.” It also specifies that flight crews perform applicable recurrent training, checking, and operational currency requirements. In addition:
• The FAA reviewed the current version of the Embraer EMB-145 Airplane Flight Manual (AFM) and the abnormal procedure for “steering system inoperative or uncommanded swerving on ground” was added to the FAA-approved AFM in revision 65, published on October 3, 2013;
• Title 14, Code of Federal Regulations Part 121, § 121.415(h)(2), requires the air carrier training program to ensure crewmembers qualify on new equipment, procedures, and techniques, which would include relevant revisions to the FSB report;
• Section 121.424(a)(1) requires all flight maneuvers and procedures outlined in part 121 Appendix E to be included in both initial and transition training; and
• Part 121 requires training of normal, abnormal or alternate operation of systems, including hydraulic systems and landing gear malfunctions.

I believe the FAA has effectively addressed Safety Recommendation A-17-6 and consider our actions complete, and I will update the Board on A-17-5 by September 30, 2021.

-From Michael P. Huerta, Administrator: The FAA will work to determine the need to facilitate the implementation of part 121 cargo operator policy and procedures into Order 1800.560, National Flight Standards Work Program Guidelines. We have established the Cargo Focus Team to provide technical support for inspectors and to provide a standardized approach to air cargo issues. In response to the guidance changes established by this group, the following Safety Assurance System Data Collection Tools related to cargo operations were updated and released on October 19, 2015:
• (6. 1.1) Training of Station Personnel;
• (6.2.4) Line Station Operations/Ground Personnel Duties;
• (6.3.1) Carriage of Cargo;
• (6.3.3) Cargo Handling Equipment, Systems, and Appliances;
• ( 4.2.1) Maintenance/Inspection Requirements;
• (4.4.5) Weight & Balance Program; and
• (3.3.3) Flight/Load Manifest/Weight & Balance Control.

-From Steve Dickson, Administrator: On August 8, 2018, the FAA published the following revised sections of FAA Order 8900.1 , Flight Standards Information Management System:
• Volume 3 Chapter 47, Section 1, Safety Assurance System: Evaluating a Certificate Holder/Applicant's Weight and Balance Control Program;
• Volume 3 Chapter 47, Section 2, Safety Assurance System: Evaluating a Certificate Holder/Applicant's Weight and Balance Program (Operations);
• Volume 3 Chapter 47, Section 3, Safety Assurance System: Evaluating a Certificate Holder/Applicant's Weight and Balance Program (Airworthiness);
• Volume 6, Chapter 2, Section 4, Safety Assurance System: Ramp and Random (Ramp) Inspections for Part 91 K, 125, 141 Operators and Part 121 and 135 Air Carriers; and
• Volume 6, Chapter 2, Section 5, Safety Assurance System: Conduct a Ramp Inspection on Cargo Loading (Including Part 125).

The revisions to Volume 3, Chapter 47, Section I and the Volume 6 revisions specifically address this recommendation, as we reported in our June 27, 2018, letter. The revisions to Volume 3, Chapter 47, Sections 2 and 3 were added for the delineation of the differences in duties between operations and airworthiness inspectors.

I believe that the FAA has satisfactorily addressed these recommendations and consider our actions complete.

-From Steve Dickson, Administrator: AC 20-174 invokes the requirement management, validation, and implementation verification processes as contained in ARP 4754A. These processes can be found in the following sections of ARP 4754A:
• Section 5.3 - Requirements Capture;
• Section 5.4 - Requirements Validation;
• Section 5.7 - Process Assurance; and
• Section 5.8 - Certification and Regulatory Authority Coordination.

I will keep the Board informed of the FAA' s progress on these safety recommendations and anticipate providing an update by February 2021.

-From Billy Nolen, Acting Administrator: The FAA continues to prioritize the publication of the Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (NPRM) for the System Safety Assessment (SSA) rule currently in its final internal coordination with an anticipated publication date by December 31, 2022, to meet the requirements of The Aircraft Certification, Safety, and Accountability Act. The part 25 amendment will address the risk from latent failures and includes control systems jams and modifications to 14 CFR § 25.671, General, and § 25.1309, Equipment, Systems, and Installations, to address the risk of wear-related failures. 

Concurrent with the SSA NPRM, the FAA will release the following new and revised draft advisory circulars (AC) for public comment: 

