CC# 201000345 - From J. Randolph Babbitt, Administrator: The Federal Aviation Administration acknowledges the Board's concerns over the use of the autopilot system in icing conditions. The FAA has carefully considered the Board's recommendation and weighed the safety benefits of such a requirement against the potential added risk that it would introduce. In evaluating this recommendation the FAA had to account for the fact that operating in icing conditions, for properly equipped and certificated aircraft is a normal operating condition, which is safely encountered daily during the winter season. In contrast, significant aircraft performance degradation or loss of control effectiveness is associated with severe icing conditions or flight for extended periods of time in heavy icing conditions. Both of these are rare conditions and not part of normal operating procedures. The FAA feels that prohibiting the use of the autopilot during normal icing operations would unnecessarily increase the risk of other forms of accidents or incidents introduced by the higher workload of manually flying the aircraft. The added attention required by the flightcrew to manually fly the aircraft may distract them from recognizing significant ice accretions, performance degradation, and proper operation of the de/anti-icing systems.
To address the Board's and FAA's shared concerns over proper use of the autopilot system in icing conditions, the FAA has taken the following actions:
• On November 1, 2006, published Safety Alert for Operators (SAFO) 06016, In-Flight Icing, Turbo Propeller Powered Airplanes (previously provided). The SAFO indicates pilots should follow approved guidance for use of the autopilot in icing conditions. The SAFO also recommends, unless authorized in the airplane flight manual, the vertical modes of the autopilot that maintain a constant rate of climb or decent or pitch should not be used. In addition, pilots should disconnect the autopilot at least once every five minutes to ensure normal airplane trim and handling qualities are maintained. To make pilots aware of this information, the SAFO recommends Directors of Safety and Directors of Operations ensure this guidance is included in their flight manuals and approved pilot training program;
• On December 31, 2007, published Advisory Circular (AC) 91-74A Pilot Guide: Flight In Icing Conditions (previously provided). This AC covers a wide spectrum of icing information including: atmospheric conditions associated with inflight icing, icing effects, protection, and detection, flight planning in an avoidance of icing, and phase of flight icing operating recommendations. The use of the autopilot guidance contained in this AC reinforces the guidance contained in SAFO 06016;
• Additionally, the FAA has issued approximately 40 Airworthiness Directives (AD) for aircraft with pneumatic de-icing boots and unpowered aileron roll controls, including an AD specific to the accident aircraft type (AD 2008-06-11). These ADs required the aircraft manufacturer to place in the Airplane Flight Manual, visual cues for recognizing severe icing, a requirement for immediate exiting severe icing conditions if encountered, and the prohibition of use of auto-pilot in severe icing conditions. We believe the actions we have taken appropriately address the Board's concerns over use of the autopilot in icing conditions and accomplish the safety gains sought by Safety Recommendation A-06-51.
I believe the FAA has effectively addressed this safety recommendation, and I consider our actions complete.

Letter Mail Controlled 11/8/2007 10:35:35 AM MC# 2070640: - From Robert A. Sturgell, Acting Administrator:  Current Federal Aviation Administration regulations (14 CFR §§ 121.363 and 135.413) place primary responsibility for the accomplishment of maintenance, preventive maintenance, and alterations with each certificate holder. Sections 121.367 and 135.425 require each certificate holder to have a maintenance program that (1) provides that all maintenance is performed in accordance with its manual, (2) competent personnel and adequate facilities are provided and, (3) each aircraft released to service is airworthy and has been properly maintained for operations in air transportation. 
Sections 121.373 and 135.431 require each certificate holder to have its own maintenance program oversight system. This is accomplished by establishing and maintaining a continuing analysis and surveillance system (CASS). The two major objectives of each certificate holder’s CASS are to identify discrepancies in the execution of its maintenance program, as well as discrepancies in program efficacy. An air carrier maintenance program must be dynamic. For example, it is not static, but it is in a constant state of continuous improvement based on discrepancies that are identified through the CASS. 
Sections 119.65(d), 121.365(a), 121.367(b), 135.423(a), and 135.425(b) require each certificate holder to have sufficient management and technical personnel who have a high level of competence, and an organization that is adequate to do the work. A certificate holder’s maintenance organization must be able to perform, supervise, manage, and amend its maintenance program; manage and guide its maintenance personnel; and provide the direction necessary to achieve the maintenance program objectives. Competent management and technical personnel and an adequate organization are significant, but often overlooked, keystones of the highest possible degree of safety in air transportation. 
In order to enjoy any degree of success, any organization, particularly an air carrier maintenance organization, must employ these four basic organizational objectives: 
1.Define the environment within which individuals conduct their tasks; 
2.Define the policies and procedures that individuals must follow and respect; 
3.Allocate the resources that individuals need in order to achieve safety and production goals; and 
4.Investigate system failures and take all remedial action necessary to avoid a repetition. 
Failure to adhere to these four organizational objectives will result, at the least, in systemic discrepancies and failures throughout an air carrier’s flight, ground, and maintenance operations. 
In response to our own root cause analysis, the FAA has been developing new public advisory material and FAA inspector guidance and training material that deal with the systems, functions, and objectives of air carrier maintenance programs. 
The FAA’s current and continuing activities in the following areas address Safety Recommendation A-07-40: 
·AC 120-16D, Air Carrier Maintenance Programs is being updated; 
·AC 120-79A, Developing and Implementing a Continuing Analysis and Surveillance System is being updated; 
·New public advisory material and inspector guidance material in the areas of maintenance management, maintenance organizations, operational control for maintenance operations, and scheduled maintenance is being developed;
·Information in FAA Order 8300.10, Volume 3, Chapter 37, Monitor Continuing Analysis and Surveillance Program Revision has been updated through the recent publication of FAA Notice 8900.7, Air Carrier Continuing Analysis and Surveillance System (enclosed);
·A six-day, formal classroom training course number 25712 on CASS, systems thinking, and scheduled maintenance for inspectors has been implemented. We have made this training mandatory for all inspectors. Currently, the course delivery is monthly and is entering its third year. The training is scheduled for a formal audit in the second quarter of Fiscal Year 2008 and will be updated based on the results of that audit; and 
·All air carriers operating under part 121 are scheduled to be included in the FAA’s structured, system-safety-based air transportation oversight system by the beginning of 2008. 
I believe that the FAA has satisfactorily responded to this safety recommendation, and I look forward to your response.

