-From Michael P. Huerta, Administrator:  The Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) reviewed both Advisory Circular (AC) l20-16E and AC 120-17A.  The FAA has revised AC 120•:16E several times to clarify on-condition maintenance programs.  On November 15, 2012, the FAA published the most recent revision to the document, which is now known as AC 120-16F.  AC 120-16F ca.1.1 be found at the following Web address: http://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/medialAdvisory_Circular/AC%20120-16F.pdf

The following areas were updated in AC 120-16F:
• Legal basis for air carrier maintenance programs;
• Air carrier maintenance program objectives;
• Responsibility for aircraft maintenance;
• Air carrier maintenance manual requirements;
• Major sections of the typical air carrier maintenance manual;
• Required maintenance organization management positions;
- Required air carrier maintenance organization structure;
- Separation of inspection and maintenance departments;
• Required inspection items (RII);
• RII procedures, standards, and limits;
• Making and keeping required records;
• Requirements for reports of major alterations and major repairs;
- Background of the continuing analysis and surveillance system (CASS);
• Basic CASS processes; and
• CASS personnel requirements.

AC 120-17 A will undergo a complete revision, which will remove the language that has led to the contradictory FAA philosophy regarding on-condition maintenance. The revision will include the removal of the following obsolete terms: ''hard time," "on-condition," and "condition monitoring." The revision will also include modem methods and techniques for the application and use of Maintenance Reliability Programs by 14 CFR part 121 and 13 5 air carriers. In order to revise the AC, the FAA requested formation of an Aviation Rulemaking Advisory Committee, which will invite the aviation industry to provide input on the revision of this AC. The FAA will select the work group in September 2013, and the targeted date for completion of work group recommendations is September 30, 2014. AC 120-17A's revision, including the public comment period, should be complete by December 31, 2015.

Once completed, the differences between AC 120-17 A and AC 120-16E regarding the FAA philosophy and use of on-condition maintenance will be more compatible with the most current accepted philosophy.

I will keep the Board informed of the FAA's progress on these safety recommendations. I will provide an update by September 30, 20i4.

