Letter Mail Controlled 10/17/2006 9:09:54 AM MC# 2060508: - From Marion C. Blakey, Administrator:  The FAA has developed a safety alert assessment plan for Safety Alerts and has begun data collection. The Safety Alert Assessment Team will use quantitative and qualitative methods of data collection and analysis to support the assessment. Review of MSAW at Terminal facilities revealed that changes in the adaptation values associated with capture boxes used by the automation near to each runway would help reduce the number of nuisance alerts. Therefore, we have made several changes that refine alert parameters for both MSAW and CA, helping to increase the reliability of each system by reducing false alarms. Those changes include: 
·Reducing approach capture box dimensions; 
·Improving general terrain map values; 
·Enhancing aircraft turn detection logic and turn rate logic; 
·Improving tracking capabilities for non-Mode-C aircraft; and 
·Adding program logic that incorporates all enhancements to more accurately predict a developing low-altitude event. 

For En Route facilities, they have now received the new automation backup system (En Route Back-up System -EBUS) which includes MSAW and CA capability consistent with the alerting contained in the primary automation system (HOST). The implementation of EBUS is expected to minimize unwarranted alerts and reduce de-sensitizing of the En Route controller when the backup automation system is being used. Once the data collection is complete, the FAA will review the results and determine if any other software changes are necessary to continue to enhance the MSAW and CA system. 

Angela Anderson, Manager, Recommendations Branch, FAA, 6/11/07  The FAA Human Factors and Engineering Group is actively engaged in a study of the human factors issues associated with MSAW and CA.  The study plan objectives are: 1.To identify any human factors issues with the existing safety alert systems, procedures, and local implementations.  2.To recommend improvements to the current alerts where necessary, based on human factors research and best practice.  This human factors study was initiated in the fall of 2006 in response to the NTSB report dated July 12, 2006, recommendation A-06-44.  We have performed a human factors analyses on hundreds of CA/MSAW alerts using data from field sites.  The study is currently in the final stages of data analysis.  We anticipate publication of the final technical report in September 2007

-From Michael P. Huerta, Administrator: Currently, pilot applicants are screened for color vision at each medical examination. The Aviation Medical Examiner (AME) Guide specifies 15 acceptable clinical screening tests: six Pseudo Isochromatic Plate tests and nine acceptable substitute instruments. Airmen who pass any of these tests may be cleared without restriction by an AME. No limit is imposed upon the number of tests attempted. Those failing clinical screening may be issued a certificate with a dual limitation, "not valid for night flight or under color signal control." These restrictions may be removed for Class III operations by successfully completing the Operational Color Vision Test (OCVT) consisting of signal light and chart reading testing at a Flight Standards District Office (FSDO). Class I and Class II operations require successful completion of the OCVT and a color vision Medical Flight Test (MFT) completed in an aircraft and in flight.

The Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) conducted a series of studies in response to this recommendation. We documented color use in the pilot operating environment, including airport and approach lighting and signaling systems and cockpit use of color. In 2011, the Federal Air Surgeon convened a workgroup to review potential revisions to the testing process to ensure cleared pilots can discriminate airport surface lighting, glide path signaling systems, and cockpit displays. In 2012, the Aerospace Human Factors Research Division completed studies examining the performance of subjects with normal and deficient color vision on clinical screening tests, computer-based precision clinical instruments, and job sample tests of discrimination of signal lights, airport approach and surface lighting using both incandescent and light emitting diode sources, and cockpit display colors. Results revealed that failing even a single clinical screening test represents a doubling of risk of inability to discriminate colors required to perform safely, as measured by the job sample tests.
The research team recommended that the Federal Air Surgeon:

Limit opportunity to pass clinical screening to a single test attempt per medical exam;
Add American Optical Company supplemental yellow-blue plates to red-green-only tests, because yellow-blue deficiency represents a safety gap in the present standard (yellow-blue deficiencies are rare but can be acquired);
Remove from the AME Guide certain tests not recommended by this research;
Require airmen failing any clinical test to pass computer-based precision testing to be cleared without restriction by an AME;
Require airmen failing precision testing to complete FSDO signal light/charting testing and/or medical flight testing as under current policy; and
Allow airmen passing flight testing in technologically advanced aircraft to be cleared for all aircraft; airmen passing an MFT in electromechanically-instrumented aircraft be cleared only for those aircraft.

Airmen clearing this process would have little risk of inability to discriminate colors used in the aircraft or airspace in which they operate. This would continue to be effective in the future, provided aircraft manufacturers continue to comply with the color palette required by § 25. I 322(a)(2) and/or recommended by AC 25-11, "Electronic Flight Deck Displays," and by General Aviation Manufacturers Association Publication #12, "Recommended Practices and Guidelines for an Integrated Cockpit/Flightdeck in a 14 CFR Part 23 Certificated Airplane." Projecting the impact of this policy using data from our research sample, we expect that 94.7 percent of pilots applying for a medical certificate would be cleared without restriction by clinical screening tests; 3 percent would be cleared without restriction following precision testing. The remainder who warrant unrestricted clearance (1 percent who pass job sample tests after failing clinical and precision testing) would be cleared by FSDO signal light/charting testing and/or medical flight testing. Approximately, 1.3 percent of all pilots would be expected to remain restricted following FSDO signal light/charting testing and/or medical flight testing.

The research team presented its results to the Federal Air Surgeon. These recommendations are currently under review, and a decision regarding the adoption and implementation will be made prior to our next update to the Board.

I will keep the Board informed of the FAA's progress on this recommendation and provide an update by January 31, 2014.

-From Michael P. Huerta, Acting Administrator: In further response to Safety Recommendation A-03-8, the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) identified certain airplane models that are more susceptible to tail strikes, and for each model, we had initiated a plan to work with manufacturers to assess the potential consequences of hidden damage under a tail strike doubler repair. In parallel with this effort, the FAA performed a detailed assessment of ongoing rulemaking and compliance activities related to structural repair practices, including the Aging Airplane Safety and Widespread Fatigue Damage Final Rules and existing mandatory structural maintenance requirements, to determine their effectiveness to mitigate circumstances identified in this safety recommendation. The FAA has concluded that these rulemaking and compliance activities, not only mitigate the circumstances surrounding the airplane crash that precipitated this safety recommendation, but also address a much broader range of continued airworthiness issues for aging airplane structure than our initial plan to assess tail strike repairs would have done. Therefore, we elected not to pursue assessment of individual tail strike repairs. The FAA finds that airworthiness standards and maintenance requirements, processes, and procedures, including the Aging Airplane Program related rule changes, satisfy the intent of Safety Recommendation A•03•8.

Many factors contribute to the continued operational safety or the transport airplane fleet. These factors include proper design, utilization, and maintenance of each aircraft. For airplanes operated under the purview of the FAA, operators must perform all FAA approved mandatory maintenance actions (e.g., inspect ions, repairs, and replacements) using tools and equipment necessary to assure completion of the work in accordance with accepted industry practices and to the level of safety intended by FAA regulations. While a one-time inspection of certain aircraft for the possibility of hidden damage may mitigate the potential hazards of hidden damage under a tail strike repair, such action does not address the essential industry reliance on employing accepted/approved maintenance practices for all aircraft and types of repairs. Further, such action does not address the risks associated with future repairs. 

Since the 1990s, the FAA has implemented several initiatives and rule changes, including associated guidance, meant to ensure the continued airworthiness of aging airplane structures. These initiatives and rule changes, referred to collectively as the Aging Airplane Program, include:
• Repair Assessment for Pressurized fuselages Final Rule (65 FR 24108);
• Aging Airplane Safety Final Rule (70 FR 55 18);
• Damage Tolerance Data for Repairs and Alterations final Rule (72 FR 70486);
• Supplemental Structural Inspection Program;
• Mandatory Modification Program;
• Corrosion Prevention and Control Program; and
• Aging Airplane Program: Widespread Fatigue Damage Final Rule (75 FR 69746)
Several of these initiatives require design approval holders (e.g., airplane manufacturers) to develop data to support operator compliance with the operational rules. From the list above. we consider two rules in the FAA's Aging Airplane Program especially important to mitigate the transport airplane fleet exposure to a similar situation leading to the China Airlines Flight CI•611 accident. The two rules are:

• Repair Assessment for Pressurized Fuselages (§§ 91.1505, 12J.L107, 125.505, and 129.107). This is an operational rule that requires operators to assess repairs affecting the fuselage pressure boundary and incorporate damage-tolerance-based inspections for those repairs into their maintenance program. [Applicable to Airbus Model A300 (excluding the -600 series), British Aerospace Model BAC 1-11 , Boeing Models 707, 720, 727, 737, and 747, McDonnell Douglas Models DC-8, DC-9/MD-80 and DC-10, Fokker Model F28, and Lockheed Model L-1011 airplanes.]
• Supplemental Inspections (§§ 121.1109 and 129.109). This is an operational rule that requires affected operators of transport airplanes (30 or more passengers; or maximum payload capacity of 7,500 pounds or greater) to incorporate damage-tolerance-based inspections for fatigue critical structure into their maintenance programs. This includes damage-tolerance-based inspections for baseline structure and for structure that has been repaired or altered. Under part 26, subpart E (Damage Tolerance Data for Repairs and Alterations rule), design approval holders are required to develop data to assist operators in obtaining damage-tolerance-based inspections for various repairs. This includes the requirement for type certificate holders to develop repair evaluation guidelines for operators' use.

In addition, we further reviewed the Aviation Safety Council's (ASC) accident report for China Airlines flight CI-611. Based on those reviews, we have concluded that transport category airplanes operated and maintained under the purview of the FAA are not exposed to similar risks as on flight CI-611. The ASC identified numerous factors that contributed to the accident (reference ASC Aviation Occurrence Report - ASC-AOR-05-02-001), indicating that the operator:
• Did not perform the repair per the manufacturer's structural repair manual;
• Did not follow mandatory maintenance requirements and normal maintenance practices;
• Missed multiple required corrosion inspection items in the area of the repair; and
• Operated the subject airplane with numerous unresolved safety deficiencies for years leading up to the accident.

The FAA has concluded that the Board's recommended airworthiness directive would not prevent an accident similar to China Airlines flight CI-611 because that operator failed to follow mandatory structural repair practices, to comply with mandatory maintenance requirements, and to rectify safety deficiencies. We have concluded from our review that the initiatives and rule changes for the Aging Airplane Program, along with existing FAA maintenance requirements, policies, and procedures, are sufficient to ensure safe operation of transport category airplanes operated under the purview of the FAA.

I believe that the FAA has effectively addressed these recommendations and I consider our actions complete.

Letter Mail Controlled 3/13/2008 11:47:15 AM MC# 2080120: Robert A. Sturgell, Acting Administrator, FAA, 3/5/08:  In our previous letter, we agreed that in the event of a tire rupture, adequate protection to the DC- 10 hydraulic system components in the wing area may not be provided from tire fragments and that appropriate corrective action would be needed. The MD- 10 and MD-11 airplanes also have a similar installation design and would also require similar corrective actions. We planned to evaluate the replacement of the wing access panel with a stronger design, which was considered to be the most effective way to protect the hydraulic systems.

We also stated that Boeing, the airplane manufacturer, was developing service bulletins to address this .work and that Boeing was conducting a common cause-and-effect (zonal)
analysis for the wing area to verify that tire debris from all other tire locations will not create an unsafe condition. Once the analysis was completed and the service bulletins were released, the FAA planned to initiate regulatory action to mandate the installation.
The following items describe the evolution of changes that were made to what the FAA
originally planned along with the accomplishments:

·In December 2003, we determined that the proposed strengthened panel would not be able to withstand the impact of large tire debris fragments that are directly in-line with the main landing gear tires. Based on the zonal analysis we determined that re-routing the number 3 hydraulic lines in the right wing and adding local shielding (Kevlar) in both wing areas would be an acceptable alternative to correct the unsafe condition;

·In January 2005, due to the long lead times to obtain Kevlar shielding material, we determined that it would be desirable to evaluate a potential alternative design, which would be to replace local number 3 hydraulic lines with increased inner thickness lines in both wing areas;

·In April 2005, through laboratory impact performance testing, we validated that the increased thickness in the number 3 hydraulic line will provide protection from tire debris;

·Boeing provided a report documenting their investigation and analysis of a tire burst at all tire positions with landing gear down. The report concluded that no further design modification would be required. In July 2007, we concurred with Boeing’s findings; and

·On September 2 1, 2007, we issued Airworthiness Directive (AD) 2007- 19- 17, which was effective on October 26, 2007. That AD requires DC-10/MD-10/MD-11 airplanes to incorporate the necessary design changes within 24 months of the effective date to protect hydraulic system lines from tire fragments in the event of a tire rupture. A copy of the AD is enclosed.

