Letter Mail Controlled 5/29/2009 10:01:20 AM MC# 2090334: - From Lynne A. Osmus, Acting Administrator: 5/14/09  Original equipment manufacturers and operators continue to use a well documented process (Maintenance Steering Group -3) to establish and revise scheduled maintenance tasks and intervals. Therefore, the FAA finds that an industry-wide evaluation of these processes is not necessary. Our December 19, 2003, letter also outlined that existing regulations and our planned activities would address Recommendations A-02-41 and -43. We continue to work on updating our policy, guidance, and information on the subject of the methodology for developing and managing scheduled maintenance tasks and intervals. We are updating airworthiness inspector training, inspector directives, advisory circulars, inspector indoctrination training, and specific training for development and management of maintenance time limitations and intervals. 
Our completed and planned actions are as follows: 
1. We implemented Continuing Analysis and Surveillance (CASS) Training Course 25712 in September 2004. This course includes basic information on determining the effectiveness of each scheduled maintenance task and its associated interval in an air carrier’s scheduled maintenance program. The methodology for determining the effectiveness of the end-play check interval is wear rate tracking. CASS requirements also extend to analyzing wear rate data and implementing corrective action if necessary. We conduct CASS training on a monthly basis. To date, 658 FAA aviation safety inspectors have completed the training; 
2. We published Advisory Circular (AC) 120-16E, Air Carrier Maintenance Programs (enclosed). We completed draft AC 120-79A, Developing and Implementing Continuing Analysis and Surveillance Systems, in August 2008, and expect to publish this revision by October 2009; 
3. We revised the CASS portion of New Aviation Safety Inspector Indoctrination Training Course 21055, during March 2005; and, 
4. We continuously update information in directives, notices, and Acs to reflect and explain new methodologies used in the development of maintenance tasks and intervals.

Letter Mail Controlled 3/31/2009 4:00:26 PM MC# 2090184: Lynne A. Osmus, Acting Administrator, FAA, 3/18/09  As stated in our December 19, 2003, letter, we find that regulations and guidance for maintenance facilities address the three elements of this recommendation. 
It is important to note that following the accident on January 31, 2000, the Federal Aviation Administration amended the repair station regulations (Title 14 Code of Federal Regulations (14 CFR) part 145 on August 6, 2001). These amended regulations became effective on April 6, 2003. Under the amended regulations, a certificated repair station must demonstrate to the FAA that it has the equipment, qualified personnel, technical data, and housing and facilities needed to accomplish work under that rating before the FAA can issue a particular rating, such as a jackscrew overhaul rating. This is consistent with the Board’s recommendation. In addition, for all work done under any of its ratings, the amended regulations require the following: 

A certificated repair station to have a detailed repair station procedures manual (14 CFR §§ 145.207 and 145.209; 
A quality control system (14 CFR § 145.211); 
Capability assessment procedures (14 CFR § 145.215); 
Specific approval for return to service procedures (14 CFR § 145.213); Maintenance record keeping procedures (14 CFR § 145.219); and 
An employee training program (14 CFR § 145.163). 
Additionally the FAA issued new guidance in Advisory Circular (AC) 145-10, Repair Station Training Program (enclosed), in July 2005 to go along with existing guidance contained in AC 145-9, A Guide for Developing and Evaluating Repair Station and Quality Control Manuals (enclosed). In September 2007 we updated inspector guidance in FAA Order 8900.1, Flight Standards Information Management System, volume 2, chapter 11, section 5, Evaluate Part 145 Repair Station Facilities and Equipment (enclosed). 
I believe that the FAA has effectively addressed this safety recommendation, and I consider our actions complete.

