CC# 201000346: - From J. Randolph Babbitt, Administrator: The Federal Aviation Administration conducted an analysis of several potential design modifications to existing jackscrew systems of the subject aircraft to include: changing the acme nut, revising the lower rotational stop, adding a second jackscrew, replacing with a hydraulic actuator or ballscrew, or installing a wear sensor. None of these potential modifications were practicable. In their January 2005 response, the Board acknowledged our determination and considered item (1) of this safety recommendation completed.

To address items (2) and (3), we identified transport category airplane models that comprised
99 percent of the Title 14 CFR Parts 121 and 135 passenger fleet for our evaluation of single-point failure modes. These airplane models included:
• Airbus A300, A310, A318, A319, A320, A321, A330, and A340;
• Boeing 727, 737, 747, 757, 767, and 777;
• Boeing (McDonnell-Douglas) DC-10 and MD-11;
• Bombardier CRJ; and
• Embraer EMB-135, EMB-140, and EMB-145.
We evaluated the horizontal stabilizer trim systems of each affected model listed above using information provided by the manufacturers. This information included:
1. Thread type (ball screw, acme screw, etc.);
2. Thread/actuation redundancy features;
3. Thread/nut/actuator failure indications;
4. Back-driving prevention method and redundancy;
5. Back-driving prevention method failure indications;
6. Actuation method;
7. Actuation safety features;
8. Trim indication; and
9. Scheduled maintenance.
For those designs found to have a potential for developing a single point failure mode due to lack of proper maintenance and excessive wear, we issued multiple Airworthiness Directives (ADs)to require additional maintenance actions. Resulting ADs include:
• Boeing 737: AD 2008-05-05;
• Boeing 747: AD 2006-10-02;
• Boeing 757: AD 2005-12-18;
• Boeing 767: AD 2008-06-06; and
• Boeing 777: AD 2009-14-06 (supersedes 2007-17-12).
For remaining designs, we found that each system was adequate in terms of its safety features, failure indications, maintenance aspects, and redundancy.
The following six airplane models have a fixed (non-trimmable) horizontal stabilizer, and are not vulnerable to the same type of failure and therefore, were not evaluated further:
• GIE Avions de Transport Regional ATR 42;
• GIE Avions de Transport Regional ATR 72;
• BAE Systems Jetstream 4101;
• Bombardier (de Havilland) DHC-8;
• Embraer EMB-120; and
• Saab 340.

I believe that the FAA has effectively addressed this safety recommendation, and I consider our actions complete.

Letter Mail Controlled 5/29/2009 10:01:20 AM MC# 2090334: - From Lynne A. Osmus, Acting Administrator:  Original equipment manufacturers and operators continue to use a well documented process (Maintenance Steering Group -3) to establish and revise scheduled maintenance tasks and intervals. Therefore, the FAA finds that an industry-wide evaluation of these processes is not necessary. Our December 19, 2003, letter also outlined that existing regulations and our planned activities would address Recommendations A-02-41 and -43. We continue to work on updating our policy, guidance, and information on the subject of the methodology for developing and managing scheduled maintenance tasks and intervals. We are updating airworthiness inspector training, inspector directives, advisory circulars, inspector indoctrination training, and specific training for development and management of maintenance time limitations and intervals. 
Our completed and planned actions are as follows: 
1. We implemented Continuing Analysis and Surveillance (CASS) Training Course 25712 in September 2004. This course includes basic information on determining the effectiveness of each scheduled maintenance task and its associated interval in an air carrier’s scheduled maintenance program. The methodology for determining the effectiveness of the end-play check interval is wear rate tracking. CASS requirements also extend to analyzing wear rate data and implementing corrective action if necessary. We conduct CASS training on a monthly basis. To date, 658 FAA aviation safety inspectors have completed the training; 
2. We published Advisory Circular (AC) 120-16E, Air Carrier Maintenance Programs (enclosed). We completed draft AC 120-79A, Developing and Implementing Continuing Analysis and Surveillance Systems, in August 2008, and expect to publish this revision by October 2009; 
3. We revised the CASS portion of New Aviation Safety Inspector Indoctrination Training Course 21055, during March 2005; and, 
4. We continuously update information in directives, notices, and Acs to reflect and explain new methodologies used in the development of maintenance tasks and intervals.

Letter Mail Controlled 6/3/2010 9:01:12 AM MC# 2100182 - From J. Randolph Babbitt, Administrator:  As part of the Federal Aviation Administration's Aging System Program, known as Enhanced Airworthiness Program for Airplane Systems (EAPAS), the Small Transport Working Group (STWG) was formed in October 2001. In January 2002, STWG was formally added to the Aging Transport Systems Rulemaking Advisory Committee (ATSRAC) as the Small Transport Aircraft Harmonization Working Group 10 (STAHWG-10). In April 2002, STAHWG- 10 was tasked the following:
1. Investigate the applicability of previous ATSRAC recommendations;
2. Identify issues unique to these (aircraft) systems and recommend appropriate actions;
And 3. Make recommendations to ATSRAC and the FAA.

These tasks were included when evaluating the airplane wiring installed on representative examples of aging airplanes. We reviewed service documents currently available to the fleet for possible additional action and reviewed existing airworthiness directives, which mandated periodic repetitive inspections for possible termination. The study and evaluations of wiring on 39 small transport category aircraft revealed conditions similar in nature to those found on large transport aircraft. As a result, these aircraft types were included in the EAPAS rulemaking. To ensure the safety of wiring systems and to reduce the chance of an in-flight fuel-tank explosion on transport category airplanes, we completed the following actions:
Issued Final Rule, Enhanced Airworthiness Program for Airplane Systems Fuel Tank Safety (EAPAS/FTS), on October 22, 2007 (copy previously forwarded). The rule became effective on December 10, 2007, and introduced new maintenance, inspection, and design criteria for airplane wiring to address conditions that put transport airplanes at risk of wire failures, smoke, and fire; Created a new part 25, subpart H as a single place in the regulations where the majority of certification rules pertaining to transport airplane wiring systems can be found; Created a new 8 25.1729 that is applicable to new type certificate and supplemental type certificate holders and applicants. It requires the development of maintenance and inspection requirements for the airplane wiring systems by analyzing the zones of their airplanes for the presence of wire damage and for the likely accumulation of contaminants; and Issued several advisory circulars (ACs).
AC-120-94 presents a curriculum for Electrical Wiring Interconnection Systems (EWIS) training and guidance for complying with 8 121.375 as applied to EWIS maintenance and inspection. Existing 5 121.375 requires that certificate holders or anyone performing maintenance have a training program; and Eleven additional ACs provide guidance on requirements contained in the EAPASIFTS final rule. In all, the EAPASIFTS final rule contains over 70 new, revised, or renumbered regulations in parts 1,21,25, 26, 91, 121, 125, and 129. This comprehensive set of design, maintenance, and inspection requirements works in harmony to ensure the safety of transport category airplanes' wiring systems.
Additionally, the FAA's Flight Controls and Mechanical Systems program conducted several research projects to address this recommendation. These research efforts include the evaluation of design and maintenance requirements for airplane mechanical systems, and the inspection and destructive testing of single element, dual load path flight control linkages. These efforts produced the technical reports listed below. A copy of each is enclosed.
1. A Rational Approach for Aging Mechanical Systems Program;
2. Aging Aircraft Evaluation of the Airbus A320 Rudder Control System;
3. Aging Mechanical Systems; and
4. Visual Inspection Results of Aged Boeing 737 and 747 Single-Element, Dual-Load Path Flight Control Components.
I believe the FAA has effectively addressed this safety recommendation by issuing the EAPASIFTS final rule, the numerous ACs and technical reports, and I consider our actions complete.

The Safety Board is pleased that there are systems that are effective in allowing access to hidden areas in narrow-body aircraft, but we are disappointed that, more than a year after the Technical Center submitted its draft report on fighting fires in inaccessible areas in aircraft, the FAA is still reviewing the report and has not published the results.  The Board reiterates its June 17, 2004 request that the FAA provide a copy of the report when it becomes available.  

The Board believes that the FAA may now have enough information to consider requiring implementation in narrow-body aircraft of procedures or airplane modifications that will provide a means for crewmembers to gain access to areas behind interior panels for the purpose of applying fire extinguishing agents, as recommended.  Pending receipt of the report on the FAA's research, and implementation of procedures or airplane modifications that provide an effective means for crewmembers to gain access to areas behind interior panels for the purpose of applying fire extinguishing agents to hidden fires, Safety Recommendation A-01-86 remains classified OPEN -- ACCEPTABLE RESPONSE.
The FAA also provided an update on a wide variety of research and other activities the FAA is pursuing related to fire-hardening of aircraft interiors, including:

1. Improved flammability requirements for thermal/acoustic insulation;
2. Guidance on fire detection and firefighting techniques, including the issuance of an advisory circular;
3. Research on the flammability of materials in inaccessible areas;
4. Development of test methods for evaluation of low-heat-release materials;
      Revisions of the standards for wiring performance and test requirements;
5. Development of certification criteria for new fire detector sensor technology; and
6. Development of arc-fault circuit breakers.

As stated in the Safety Board's June 17, 2004, letter to the FAA about this recommendation, these are all excellent efforts that the Board supports.  Several of these activities are directly responsive to other safety recommendations issued by the Board.  However, although these efforts may ultimately help reduce the number of in flight fires, they do not meet the full intent of this specific recommendation, which asks for the development and implementation of procedures or airplane modifications that will provide an effective means for crewmembers to gain access (emphasis added) to areas behind interior panels for the purpose of applying extinguishing agent to hidden fires.  They are, however, important advances in safety that the Board urges the FAA to continue.

-From Michael P. Huerta, Acting Administrator: On October 12, 2010, the FAA issued Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (NPRM), Air Ambulance and Commercial Helicopter Operations, Part 91 Helicopter Operations, and Part 135 Aircraft Operations; Safety Initiatives and Miscellaneous Amendments. The comment period closed on January 10, 2011. The FAA received the comments submitted by the Board and is reviewing those comments as part of the rulemaking process. The NPRM proposes to add requirements for:
• A load manifest for all aircraft operated under part 135 (§ 135.63(c));
• Radio altimeters for all helicopters operated under part 135 (§ 135.160);
• Initial and recurrent pilot testing to include procedures for aircraft handling in flat-light, whiteout, and brownout conditions (§ 135.293(a)(9));
• Competency checks that include a demonstration of the pilot's ability to maneuver solely by reference to instruments (§ 135.293(c));
• The use of helicopter terrain awareness and warning systems in helicopter air ambulance operations (§ 135.605);
• An FAA-approved procedure for conducting pre-flight risk analyses when conducting helicopter air ambulance operations (§ 135.615);
• An operations control center and operations control specialists for helicopter air ambulance operations (§ 135.617); and
• A briefing by the pilot in command or designated crewmember to medical personnel prior to each helicopter air ambulance operation (§ 135.619).

Additionally, the NPRM defines helicopter air ambulance in § 135.601(b)(l) as operations by a part 135 certificate holder.
I will keep the Board informed of the FAA's progress on these recommendations, and provide an updated response by March 31, 2013.

