-From Kathryn Thomson, Deputy Administrator: The FAA continues working towards the AWD Web Server Project implementation. According to the project plan, installation of AWD to the following six test sites is scheduled to begin by August 31, 2023: 

1) Atlanta Air Route Traffic Control Center (ARTCC) (ZTL); 
2) Atlanta Terminal Radar Approach Control (TRACON) (A80); 
3) Atlanta Hartsfield International Airport Traffic Control Tower (ATCT) (ATL); 
4) Salt Lake City ARTCC (ZLC); 
5) Salt Lake City TRACON (S56); and 
6) Salt Lake City International Airport ATCT (SLC). 

We anticipate testing for the AWD Web Server implementation to continue through the remainder of CY23 and the IOC to occur by March 31, 2024. The National Deployment Site installation phase, designed for 61 additional air traffic control facilities, is anticipated to occur by January 1, 2025. Additionally, the Integrated Terminal Weather System, which facilitates a common situational awareness of severe weather phenomena, will undergo a technical update to ensure continued graphical and alpha-numerical/text weather generation, dissemination, and display of the terminal area. 

The FAA, through a contract with the National Center for Atmospheric Sciences (NCAR), has developed a turbulence “nowcast” called the Graphical Turbulence Guidance-Nowcast (GTGN). GTGN provides rapidly updated, short-term (15 min) turbulence nowcasts for tactical route decisions in the cockpit and by airline dispatch operations. A beta version of this product is currently available from NCAR through FAA-funded support for airlines and other stakeholders. The FAA and National Weather Service (NWS) are transitioning GTGN to NWS, currently scheduled to be operational in CY25.

In response to the Board’s May 4, 2022, letter, we would like to offer clarification of the proposed system infrastructure. In the current design for work package 1 (WP1), GTGN for AWD won’t be provided by Common Support Services-Weather/NexGen Weather Processor (NWP), which is the contractor provider of weather data, products, and imagery within the National Airspace System. We expect to receive graphical turbulence products from the NWS. At this time, we do not have requirements in our agreement with the NWS to obtain GTGN. The Baseline NextGen Weather will provide the hourly updated Graphical Turbulence Guidance (GTG) System Product, Generation 3, on AWD. 

Initially, for the WP1 phase of the NWP, the graphical turbulence products will be limited to the clear air turbulence provided by the NWS. Follow-on capability will include mountain wave GTG to the AWD, provided by NWS. We expect the Investment Analysis Readiness Decision, a significant milestone for the project defining high-level concepts and preliminary requirements, by June 30, 2026.

