-From Michael P. Huerta, Administrator: It is the Federal Aviation Administration's (FAA) goal to require Safety Management Systems (SMS) for all Title 14, Code of Federal Regulations Part 135 operators, including HEMS operators. However, due to staffing limitations, a HEMS-specific SMS rule cannot be addressed at this time. The FAA needs to focus on completing the implementation of the Safety Assurance System (SAS), transitioning the SMS Pilot Project to a permanent SMS Voluntary Program, and preparing the inspectors and part 121 certificate holders for the mandated SMS rule (part 5).

Although a SMS HEMS rule is not pending in the near-term, the FAA recently issued the Helicopter Air Ambulance, Commercial Helicopter, and Part 91 Helicopter Operations Final Rule, published on February 21 , 2014 (79 FR 9932). This final rule addresses the four components of an SMS program (safety policy, safety risk management, safety assurance, and safety promotion). Section 135.617 requires each HEMS operator to establish and document, in its operations manual, an FAA-approved preflight risk analysis program. A preflight risk analysis provides certificate holders with the means to assess and mitigate risk and make determinations regarding the flight's safety before launch. The HEMS final rule also includes the following:
• § 135.603, which requires the pilot in command (PIC) to hold an instrument rating;
• § 135.605, which requires HEMS aircraft to be equipped with Helicopter Terrain Avoidance System (HTAWS);
• § 135.609, which requires more stringent visual flight rules (VFR) weather minimums in uncontrolled airspace;
• § 135.611 , which requires instrument flight rules (IFR) operations at locations without weather reporting;
• § 135.613, which establishes approach/depart IFR transitions between takeoff and landing areas;
• § 135.615, which requires obstacle clearance analysis for VFR routes; and
• § 135.621, which requires safety briefings of medical personnel.

The FAA is developing requirements for participation in the voluntary SMS program. Since joining in 2009, HEMS operators have been a significant part of the SMS voluntary program. There are 21 HEMS operators, up from 18, in the SMS voluntary program, which operate 82 percent of the HEMS aircraft. We expect the number of operators and fleet percentage to continue rising, and we will continue to reach out to the HEMS industry to encourage voluntary participation. These enhancements, among others, will foster safety practices and programs in the HEMS industry.

I will keep the Board informed of the FAA's progress on this safety recommendation and provide an updated response by December 2015.

-From Billy Nolen, Acting Administrator: As mentioned in our previous letters, the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) continues to work with the International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO) and our international partners to:

 Better define the Global Aviation Distress and Safety System (GADSS) and Location of an Aircraft in Distress Repository;

 Research Space-Based Automatic (SBA) Dependent Surveillance – Broadcast (ADS-B);

 Research SBA ADS-B for Low Frequency-Underwater Location Device;

 Define Timely Recovery of Flight Data (TRFD);

 Update Minimum Operational Performance Standards (MOPS) in European Organisation for Civil Aviation Equipment Document ED-112B, MOPS for Crash Protected Airborne Recorder Systems;

 Develop an advisory circular (AC) on TRFD once ED-112B is updated;

 Modify RTCA DO-260C, MOPS for 1090 MHz Extended Squitter ADS-B and Traffic Information Services-Broadcast, to better accommodate Autonomous Distress Tracking (ADT);

 Participate in writing an ICAO document to provide guidance for operators wishing to install ADT as well as TRFD;

 Develop an ICAO GADSS AC; and

 Improve industry knowledge and understanding of ADT and TRFD.

The results of this work will inform the FAA on how this technology can be leveraged to improve safety by working to better harmonize our regulations with ICAO standards and recommended practices.

The FAA also initiated a rulemaking (2120-AL92) that would propose newly-manufactured aircraft to be equipped with cockpit voice recorders (CVRs) capable of recording for 25 hours, along with operational rules that would require the use of those CVRs when installed. Furthermore, the FAA is launching an Aviation Rulemaking Committee (ARC) that will provide a forum for the U.S. aviation community to discuss, prioritize, and provide recommendations to the FAA concerning requirements for the installation of existing, new, and upgraded investigative technologies that affect applicable airworthiness standards and operating rules. The ARC will also specifically consider the Board’s safety recommendations as part of its discussions.

I will keep the Board informed of the FAA’s progress on Safety Recommendations A-15-1 through 4, -7, and -8, and provide an update by May 31, 2024.

